WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.440
It runs from the capital, Kabul, to the second
biggest city, Kandahar.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.860
It was the cornerstone of the US strategy
to rebuild Afghanistan after the invasion in 2001.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:19.400
It cost over $200 million to build and hundreds
of lives were lost defending it.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.660
Despite all that the Kabul to Kandahar Highway,
today, is broken.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.080
A 2016 audit report found that the road was

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.420
and if it becomes impassable...

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.000
To understand how a road this significant
and this costly can be falling apart, you

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.460
have to ask:
Where did the US go wrong in Afghanistan?

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.310
Just weeks after 9/11, the US invaded Afghanistan,
where Al Qaeda planned the attacks.

00:00:57.310 --> 00:01:01.420
They were sheltered by the Taliban, who controlled
the Afghan government at the time.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.780
Both groups were driven out of Kabul in a
matter of months, so the US strategy soon

00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:10.049
shifted from combat, to stabilizing and rebuilding
the country.

00:01:10.049 --> 00:01:12.939
But Afghanistan is a difficult place to control
and rebuild.

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:15.149
It’s mountainous, and mostly rural.

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:19.100
The population is fractured among several
ethnic groups and local communities often

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.740
operated autonomously.

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:26.680
To make matters worse, there were only 50
kilometers of paved roads in 2002, which meant

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:29.299
most of these communities were isolated.

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:33.530
The US decided to change that by rebuilding
The Ring Road that was partially built by

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.939
the Soviet Union in the 60s but had been destroyed
by decades of war.

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:43.439
Starting with the Kabul to Kandahar section,
the US and several other countries pledged

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:45.289
$1.5 Billion dollars to the Ring Road.

00:01:45.289 --> 00:01:51.299
It would run in a 3,200 kilometer loop, connecting
Afghanistan’s 4 biggest cities - essentially

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:54.020
tying these communities together.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:58.179
And it was showing promise: Trade circulated
through more places, and medical services

00:01:58.179 --> 00:01:59.540
reached more people.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.930
It gave the new government in Kabul more legitimacy
around the country.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:06.599
The Ring Road also allowed the US and NATO
military to send troops and supplies around

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.160
the country faster, so they could keep the
Taliban in check.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.840
“Where the roads end in Afghanistan, the
Taliban begin."

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:17.780
"In other words, roads promote enterprise."

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.320
"Enterprise provides hope."

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.970
"Hope is what defeats this ideology of darkness."

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.960
But the US didn’t finish the job.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.860
In 2003, the US invaded Iraq.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.440
And Afghanistan become second priority.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.410
Funding, reconstruction, resources, and experienced
leadership, including generals and diplomats

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.660
were all diverted to the war in Iraq.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:43.780
The Ring Road was far from complete yet reconstruction
funding was cut by $1.2 billion a few years later.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.660
The US preoccupation with Iraq gave the Taliban
an opening to return, and they seized it.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:54.440
When you look at the Taliban activity in the
region from 2004 through 2009, you can see

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.670
it escalate.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:59.920
Draw the Ring Road, and you can see where
those activities are concentrated.

00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:07.720
They set up ambushes, laid roadside bombs,
took hostages, killed US soldiers and road

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:08.930
construction crews.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:13.990
By 2008, the Taliban had taken back significant
territory, especially in the south and east,

00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.870
along the Kabul to Kandahar highway.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:19.400
Afghanistan was in a full-blown insurgency.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.170
"Afghanistan is not lost, but for several years
it has moved backwards."

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:29.920
"There is no imminent threat of the government
being overthrown, but the Taliban has gained momentum."

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.060
"In short, the status quo is not sustainable."

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:37.790
In 2009, President Barack Obama decided to
recommit to the war in Afghanistan.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.680
He sent thousands of troops in what was called
The Surge.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.850
The US and NATO made some progress in the
south.

00:03:43.850 --> 00:03:47.780
But it quickly became clear that the Taliban
would not be easily defeated.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.970
The more troops deployed to Afghanistan, the
more the Taliban launched attacks.

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:57.650
With the military struggling to clear territory,
it became nearly impossible to rebuild roads,

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.300
as the Taliban continued to attack road-crews.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.940
The degraded security environment has made this

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:09.140
the most dangerous project our company
has attempted. 21 killed. 51 wounded. And 4 missing.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.000
This forced construction companies to hire
security,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.010
which caused budgets to skyrocket.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:20.980
Like this road from Khost to Pakitia, which
cost almost $5M per mile mostly because of security.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.300
But 18 months later, time was up.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.000
President Obama announced that he’d start bringing
troops back.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.280
"After this initial reduction, our troops will
continue coming home

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.040
at a steady pace, as Afghan security

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.400
forces move into the lead.

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.120
Our mission will change from combat to support.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:44.040
As US troops withdrew, they left behind oversight
of infrastructure projects, including roads.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.570
In 2012, USAID, cut funding for road construction.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:52.340
And even after the US and partnering countries
spent $3 Billion dollars on it, the Ring road

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.710
was never completed.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.540
Road-building and maintenance became the responsibility
of the Afghan government,

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:57.680
which was crippled

00:04:57.680 --> 00:04:58.970
by corruption.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:03.100
Experts estimate that billions of dollars
have been lost to corruption in Afghanistan.

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:08.931
In 2015, with only about 11,000 US troops
in country, mostly in the major cities, the

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.040
Taliban swept back through Afghanistan.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:15.320
In 2017, they controlled almost half the country
-- that’s more territory than they’ve

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:16.600
had since 2001.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.210
And that includess large sections of the Ring Road.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.750
And that’s one of the main reasons why the
road is in dire shape.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.420
According to a 2016 inspection

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:33.140
The US has no plans to give rebuilding Afghanistan
a third chance.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:37.020
In 2017, President Trump committed more troops
but made it very clear:

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.260
“We are not nation-building again."

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.060
"We are killing terrorists.“

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.620
As the Ring Road continues to deteriorate,
it's no longer a symbol of the US efforts

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:53.200
to rebuild Afghanistan, instead it serves
as a reminder of the job that was never finished.

